mouse: acme's three buttons, said in sys's verbs

A button means one thing in every pane: the shell's, ed's and fm's.
Each is a verb sys has already.

	left     this     c       the text becomes cur, and every view on
	                          cur goes there; the eye stays
	right    look     p, o    open what it names, and the eye goes there
	middle   do       p -a    hand it to the shell, at its prompt

The case, carried through: the shell in a frame prints sh/o:24, as
grep -n prints a hit.

	left     cur is sh/o at line 24: ed -f shows the line, in the lens
	         it shows, and fm -f stands in sh on o
	right    the same, and the eye goes to ed, on line 24
	middle   the path, whole, lands at the shell's prompt after what is
	         typed there, git log -p; Return runs it

In a frame, the frame is where it lands
  A frame's panes carry their slots' names, @slot (`studio/frame`), and
  the three verbs name theirs: e reaches ed, o a folder's fm, and p -a
  the shell. They ask tmux for the pane by its name, not studio, so the
  rule is one: the pane named for the verb, else a pane of its own. ed
  and fm run with -f, following cur, so opening a thing there is saying
  it and moving the eye: nothing opens beside, and the frame keeps its
  shape. A plain window names no pane, so there a verb opens one below,
  in the stack beside the shell (`tmux/conf:/main-vertical/`).

Look never runs
  o shows a thing, and the shell runs one: a script is text, so a right
  click opens it in ed, and a middle click hands it to the prompt, where
  only Return runs it. o ran what was executable until this, which made
  a tap on ./w a run of sudo and rm -rf.

The click is the word, or the selection it falls in
  As in acme, a right or a middle click inside a selection takes the
  selection: ed's, or one copy mode holds. One word is resolved to what
  it names, its punctuation stripped; several are a phrase, which p -a
  hands on as it came, for it is a command, not a name.

The text goes in as a paste
  p -a pastes rather than types: a selection's lines come joined, and a
  program that asks for brackets, bash among them, takes the paste as
  text and never as its keys, so nothing in it runs. It goes only to a
  shell at its prompt, unless the pane is the one you marked: a program
  there would read typed text as its keys.

What is where
	gesture    tmux/conf, ed's view, sys/view    which text, which button
	resolve    sh/c                              text to an address
	dispatch   sh/o, sh/e, sh/p -a               an address to a program,
	                                             or to the frame's slot
	views      ed -f, fm -f                      follow cur
  Nothing new runs: no plumber, no socket, no table but o's
  (`design/plumbing`). A view hands its clicks on as ideas: fm hears a
  right click as enter, its own open, which goes into a folder and opens
  a file, and ed's outline hears it as source on the item's line. A key
  the screen shows is a button: a left click on fm's chip, t layout,
  presses t, one on the layout not in use turns to it, and one on a lens
  named on ed's bar turns to that lens (`design/lens`).

Given up
  ed's middle click no longer pastes X's selection: shift and a middle
  click is the terminal's own paste, under any program, and ctrl-v
  pastes the clipboard. acme's chords, 1-2 cut and 1-3 paste, are not
  here: tmux hands a chord only as one button's press and the other's
  release (`todo/03-studio/05-keys`). Nor is look's search for a word
  that names nothing: p ends at its git check.
